data.content:
## What breaks in .NET 11

In .NET 10, the Cosmos DB provider already deprecated synchronous I/O but allowed you to opt back in (via `EnableSyncOverAsync()` or similar configuration). **In .NET 11, that opt-in is removed entirely.** The Cosmos DB SDK is async-only, and EF Core no longer wraps it with sync-over-async shims.

Every synchronous call in your repository will throw an **`InvalidOperationException`** at runtime:

| Sync (breaks) | Async (replacement) |
|---|---|
| `.ToList()` | `.ToListAsync()` |
| `.First()` | `.FirstAsync()` |
| `.Count()` | `.CountAsync()` |
| `.SaveChanges()` | `.SaveChangesAsync()` |

### Migrated code

```csharp
public class ProductRepository
{
    private readonly CosmosDbContext _context;

    public async Task<List<Product>> GetAllAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.ToListAsync();
    }

    public async Task<Product> GetByIdAsync(int id)
    {
        return await _context.Products.FirstAsync(p => p.Id == id);
    }

    public async Task<int> GetCountAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.CountAsync();
    }

    public async Task SaveAsync(Product product)
    {
        _context.Products.Add(product);
        await _context.SaveChangesAsync();
    }
}
```

**Key points:**
- Remove any `EnableSyncOverAsync()` calls from your `DbContext` configuration — the API no longer exists.
- All callers up the chain must propagate `async/await` (controllers, services, etc.).
- `Add()` stays synchronous — it only tracks in-memory. The actual I/O happens in `SaveChangesAsync()`.
